<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Neuberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1379">1379</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Neuberg was an agreement of 9 September 1379, signed at Neuberg Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, by which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> brothers Albert III and Leopold III divided their lands. Albert kept the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> proper, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-line">Albertine line</a>, while Leopold received Styria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and the western lands, founding the Leopoldine line. The Habsburg lands were reunited under Maximilian I in 1490.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-neuberg.jpg" alt="Treaty of Neuberg" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Captain Blood · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HRR_14Jh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Albert and Leopold were the younger brothers of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iv-duke-of-austria">Rudolf IV of Austria</a>, who on the death of their father, Duke Albert II, in 1358 took the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, held in personal union with it under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgenberg-pact">Georgenberg Pact</a> of 1186, and of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> with the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. Rudolf raised himself to archduke through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/privilegium-maius">Privilegium Maius</a> and acquired the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> from the last Meinhardiner countess Margaret in 1363. On his early death in 1365 he left his brothers lands stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> to the dynasty's original Further Austrian possessions in the west. His house law of 1364, the Rudolfinische Hausordnung, had decreed their joint rule, but they soon fell out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Albert and his heirs kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> proper, the later Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, with the Traungau and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzkammergut">Salzkammergut</a>. Leopold became sole ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, which then included Pitten and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiener-neustadt">Wiener Neustadt</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, enlarged since 1374 by the Windic March and territory in the March of Istria, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and Further Austria, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> and acquisitions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a>. Whatever their lands, all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rulers were to keep the Austrian archducal title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under Leopold's sons his lands were divided again, into Inner Austria (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> and the Littoral) and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and the Further Austrian lands, ruled from 1406 by his youngest son, Duke Frederick IV. The split weakened the dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-of-germany">Albert V of Austria</a>, of the Albertinian line, was elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1438 but died a year later, and his line ended with the death of his son Ladislaus the Posthumous in 1457. Its lands passed to Frederick V of Inner Austria, of the Leopoldian line, Albert's successor as King of the Romans and from 1452 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, and in 1490 Archduke Sigismund handed Tyrol and Further Austria to Frederick's son Maximilian I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Neuberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Neuberg</b> of 1379 divided the family's possessions, giving the western territories, together with Tyrol and Inner Austria, to Leopold III; these western lands became known as Further Austria.</blockquote><a href="/state/further-austria">Further Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Neuberg</b> of 1379 gave Leopold III the Habsburg lands south and west of Austria, including Styria, Carinthia and Carniola.</blockquote><a href="/state/inner-austria">Inner Austria</a></li></ul></section>
